\ cv8b-klookup.4 - the whole dictionary lookup as one word (Iteration 57),
\ for the byte-header system with kfast and kinput; loaded after
\ cv8b-kinput.4 for designs with the gene klookup. X8 words: the kernel's
\ word gets the body.
\
\ On seed 13's fastest design FIND and SEARCH-WORDLIST were still 28-31%
\ of the parse and corpus workloads' dispatches - FIND's loop over the
\ search order, SEARCH-WORDLIST8's own stack work - around the opcodes
\ THREAD-FIND and HASH. (FIND) is FIND's search with what it reads given:
\ NAMEBUF, CONTEXT and #ORDER @; the gene makes it a format-10 opcode, which
\ does the search order, NAME>BUF8, HASH, THREAD-FIND, NAME>8 and the
\ immediate bit in one dispatch.

: (FIND) ( c-addr nb ctx n --- c-addr 0 | xt 1 | xt -1 )
\ The kernel's FIND, step for step: the last wordlist not searched twice,
\ then each from the top of the search order down. nb is unused here -
\ SEARCH-WORDLIST fills NAMEBUF; the opcode fills it itself.
  ROT DROP SWAP >R
  DUP 1 > IF DUP 1- CELLS R@ + DUP @ SWAP 1 CELLS - @ = IF 1- THEN THEN
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    1- >R DUP COUNT R> R@ SWAP >R R@ CELLS + @ SEARCH-WORDLIST
    DUP IF R> DROP R> DROP ROT DROP EXIT THEN
    DROP R>
  REPEAT R> DROP ;

: FIND8 ( c-addr --- c-addr 0 | xt 1 | xt -1 )
  NAMEBUF CONTEXT #ORDER @ (FIND) ;

\ Number conversion: NUMBER?, >NUMBER and DIGIT? were 12-21% of parse and
\ corpus - ~40 dispatches a digit. (>NUMBER) is >NUMBER with BASE @ given,
\ for the opcode; its Forth body is the kernel's own loop (calling >NUMBER
\ would recurse - >NUMBER8 takes >NUMBER's body), reading BASE itself.

: (>NUMBER) ( ud c-addr u base --- ud2 c-addr2 u2 )
  DROP
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
   -1 + >R COUNT DIGIT? 0= IF R> 1+ SWAP -1 + SWAP EXIT THEN
   SWAP >R >R SWAP BASE @ UM* ROT BASE @ * 0 SWAP D+ R> 0 D+ R> R>
  REPEAT ;

: >NUMBER8 ( ud c-addr u --- ud2 c-addr2 u2 )
  BASE @ (>NUMBER) ;

: WORD8 ( c --- addr )
\ The kernel's WORD, HERE once instead of three times.
  PARSE HERE DUP >R PLACE BL R@ COUNT + C! R> ;
